# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the panel-on leg (CI: "TSan with Qt").
#
# Qt is not instrumented. Its own synchronisation - QMutex, QSemaphore, the event loop's wake-ups,
# the blocking queued invoke - is invisible to ThreadSanitizer, so a hand-off through it looks
# like a data race in whichever of our frames stands on either side of it. Each line below names
# one such hand-off, or one library whose intercepted calls (memcpy, strlen, pthread) must not be
# judged, and nothing else: the seam itself (WormLib::Mailbox) is a std::mutex the sanitiser sees
# whole, and a race there is a real one this leg exists to catch.

# Libraries the sanitiser did not build: what they call into libc is theirs.
called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6Gui.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6Widgets.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6Test.so.6
called_from_lib:libQt6DBus.so.6
called_from_lib:libqxcb.so
called_from_lib:libglib-2.0.so.0
called_from_lib:libfontconfig.so.1
called_from_lib:libxcb.so.1

# The blocking hand-offs across the Qt thread: a window built on it for the tick thread
# (PanelLib::open, the constructor reading the body snapshot handed over), closed and deleted on
# it at the tick thread's request (PanelLib::close), the application asked to quit (PanelLib::stop).
# Qt's BlockingQueuedConnection is the happens-before; the sanitiser cannot see it.
race:PanelLib::open
race:PanelLib::close
race:PanelLib::stop
race:PanelWindow::PanelWindow
race:PanelWindow::~PanelWindow
